"comparison": "Both JPMorgan and Bank of America disclose the use of foreign currency-denominated debt and the resulting foreign currency adjustments, but they apply them to different balance sheet items. JPMorgan attributes changes primarily to 'foreign currency adjustments' affecting 'goodwill' (an intangible asset). In contrast, Bank of America explicitly links its foreign currency-denominated debt ($43.8 billion) to 'total long-term debt' and notes the use of foreign currency contracts to convert such debt, rather than linking it to goodwill or net investment hedges in the same manner. While both acknowledge foreign currency adjustments in their financial reporting, the specific asset class and accounting mechanism differ significantly.",
