"comparison": "JPMorgan and Citigroup both identify foreign currency adjustments as a primary driver of changes in specific financial metrics, but the underlying subject matter differs. JPMorgan's adjustment relates to 'goodwill' carrying amounts. Citigroup, conversely, uses foreign currency instruments (forwards, swaps, debt) specifically to hedge 'net investments in several non-U.S.-dollar-functional-currency foreign subsidiaries,' with the resulting translation adjustments recorded in Accumulated Other Comprehensive Income (AOCI). JPMorgan's disclosure does not mention net investment hedging strategies for goodwill, whereas Citigroup's is explicitly a hedging strategy for equity investments.",
